WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.120
Russell’s back was pressed against the wall, his&nbsp;
shuddering hands gripping the fire axe. The low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.680
emergency lighting filling the corridor indicated&nbsp;
the lockdown was still in effect. Although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:16.560
that was simultaneously a blessing and a curse.&nbsp;
Sure, it meant that… thing couldn’t get out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.660
hopefully. But it also meant that Russell was&nbsp;
stuck in this wing of the facility with it.&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.940
He had no idea if anyone else was left. He&nbsp;
hadn’t seen what had happened to Carpenter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.280
Maybe it got him, or perhaps he had managed&nbsp;
to give it the slip at the last second. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.620
was no way of knowing for sure. It had&nbsp;
all started so simply, so innocently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:44.220
and with such noble intentions. If he had known it&nbsp;
would lead to all this madness, Russell would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.620
never put his name down on the request form.
The SCP Foundation had been working on a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.640
to neutralize or cure SCP-610 for what felt like&nbsp;
forever. To one team of scientists, the infamously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.820
so-called Flesh That Hates was a fascinating&nbsp;
organism. It was a highly-contagious Sarkic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:09.120
skin disease, isolated entirely within a small&nbsp;
area of Siberia. That on its own wasn’t enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.460
to pique the interest of the research team led by&nbsp;
Doctor Carpenter, including his hand-picked star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:20.640
Researchers Russell and David, along with their&nbsp;
assistant Clennon and Doctor Bottin, an expert on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.580
infectious diseases who had recently joined their&nbsp;
efforts to provide his specialist knowledge. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:32.820
did captivate all of them, however, was what&nbsp;
SCP-610 did when it infected a human being.&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.920
The disease would trigger aggressive and&nbsp;
uncontrollable mutations within its infected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.020
and could virtually rewrite their entire&nbsp;
physiology in a heartbeat. The Flesh That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.420
Hates was capable of transforming a person’s&nbsp;
body into a horrifying, fleshy mass of limbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:54.240
and matter that barely resembled a human being&nbsp;
anymore. And in addition, the infected individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.240
would retain some level of awareness, attacking&nbsp;
any uninfected person with extreme hostility.&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.940
There had long been chatter among some other&nbsp;
research teams studying SCP-610 about devising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.920
a way to weaponize the disease. If they could&nbsp;
somehow create a variant that didn’t turn people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.540
into monsters, or at least caused the fleshy&nbsp;
abominations to die shortly after mutation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:21.120
then the Foundation or another interested&nbsp;
party could deploy the Flesh That Hates as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.340
a biological weapon. Doctor Carpenter had&nbsp;
long abhorred the idea, instead searching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.580
for a way to completely reverse the effects of an&nbsp;
SCP-610 infection. It may have been idealistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.440
perhaps even naïve by some standards, but&nbsp;
the rest of his team was behind him in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.840
pursuit of that noble goal. Although not one of&nbsp;
them knew it would soon cost them their lives…&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.520
Hitting speed bump after speed bump in their&nbsp;
research, the team were beginning to lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.920
hope. Researcher David was the most outspoken&nbsp;
in how tired he was of seeing the same results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.920
yelling in frustration that the Flesh could not&nbsp;
be cured by conventional means. However, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.160
that outburst that gave Russell the idea. They&nbsp;
had tried treating SCP-610 like a common virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.280
as if it was any other form of disease, largely&nbsp;
thanks to Doctor Bottin’s input. And granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.900
that approach had taught them a lot about the&nbsp;
Flesh’s ability to contaminate a subject from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.920
a single cell. But this infection&nbsp;
was anomalous through and through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.640
could the solution to eradicating it for good&nbsp;
lie not in a medical cure, but in another SCP?&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.860
Russell brought this hypothesis to the rest of the&nbsp;
research team, and each of them had suggestions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.480
for other anomalies they could potentially&nbsp;
use to cure a subject of the Flesh That Hates.&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.000
“We could get SCP-049 to take a look at&nbsp;
an infected patient,” Clennon suggested.&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.540
“Might work,” Carpenter mused.&nbsp;
“Although even if he cures someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.520
he might also kill them. He has his whole deal&nbsp;
about ridding the world of ‘The Pestilence’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.200
could prove to be a problem.”
“There’s always 682,” David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.200
said dryly. “Have the Reptile eat up all the&nbsp;
SCP-610 infectees, and viola. Problem solved.”&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.300
“Oh, real helpful, David,” Researcher Russell&nbsp;
retorted. “And what are we going to do when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.280
SCP-682 adapts to the Flesh? You really want&nbsp;
an infected, immortal lizard on the loose?”&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.980
“Gentlemen, please,” Doctor Bottin spoke&nbsp;
up. “We’re forgetting one obvious option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.100
I propose we submit a joint formal request&nbsp;
to use SCP-914 for this experiment.”&nbsp;

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:28.440
Unanimously, the group all agreed that this&nbsp;
was the best course of action. SCP-914,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:34.440
or ‘The Clockworks’, was a giant machine with two&nbsp;
booths marked ‘Input’ and ‘Output’. Although the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:40.140
Foundation still couldn’t fully comprehend exactly&nbsp;
how, SCP-914 seemed to take any object placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:45.900
within it and disassemble, recreate, improve or&nbsp;
destroy the item depending on its current setting.&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.760
There was one issue, however. A prior&nbsp;
failed experiment wherein a D Class was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.220
placed in SCP-914 had led to some pretty&nbsp;
disastrous consequences. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.740
ever since no organic matter was to be placed&nbsp;
within the Clockworks; a rule that the team’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.660
experiment with the Flesh would breach. Still,&nbsp;
they pressed on, each of the five researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:12.720
signing their request to utilize SCP-914 before&nbsp;
it was submitted to the Foundation higher-ups.&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.960
Within hours, the request was denied. But the team&nbsp;
were determined that SCP-914 could be the key to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.900
solving the Foundation’s long-standing issue with&nbsp;
SCP-610. Doctor Carpenter repealed the decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.100
urging the higher-ups to reconsider their&nbsp;
decision. He argued that this flouting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.500
the ‘no organic matter’ rule was necessary to&nbsp;
potentially rid the world of the Flesh That Hates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:40.560
After a few further days of consideration,&nbsp;
the request was finally, fatefully granted…&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.400
The team went to work immediately,&nbsp;
requisitioning two test subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.100
both of which had suffered SCP-610 infections&nbsp;
and were horribly mutated. The pair of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.200
were deemed a potential hazard to every member&nbsp;
of Foundation staff on-site, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.580
the entire wing of the facility where the&nbsp;
Clockworks was housed had to be evacuated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:04.800
for safety. Dressed in biohazard suits to&nbsp;
protect themselves, the researchers used an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.800
electric cattle prod to coax the first Flesh&nbsp;
specimen into the Input chamber of SCP-914.&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.260
This was the test run, they wanted to see if the&nbsp;
Clockworks would actually be able to affect those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:22.140
infected with SCP-610 at all. Plus, they needed&nbsp;
a failsafe just in case something went wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:27.480
and luckily the machine had just the thing.&nbsp;
Russell asked Clennon to set the Clockworks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:33.840
to ‘Rough’, and with a click and whir of its&nbsp;
numerous gears and gyros, SCP-914 came to life.&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.400
Instantly, the infected test subject was&nbsp;
completely obliterated. The setting had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.020
reduced it to atoms, disintegrating it on&nbsp;
such a microscopic level that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.760
nothing left in the Output chamber. The second&nbsp;
infected creature seemed to bristle with anger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.800
but didn’t attack or become aggressive as&nbsp;
it too was prodded towards the Clockworks.&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.340
Next, perhaps out of fear that their theory might&nbsp;
be wrong, the team agreed to set SCP-914 to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.980
‘1:1’ setting. Sure enough, the machine recreated&nbsp;
another instance of a specimen infected with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:10.260
Flesh That Hates, replacing the previous one.
“We all know we’re stalling,” Researcher David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.400
piped up. “Let’s do what we came here&nbsp;
for, set the Clockworks to ‘Coarse’.”&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.500
Nervously, receiving a nod from Doctor Carpenter,&nbsp;
Clennon stepped forward to switch the dial on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:25.800
SCP-914 to the Coarse setting. This was the&nbsp;
primary reason the team had all agreed to use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.900
machine on a Flesh specimen; this setting could&nbsp;
disassemble any item placed in the Input booth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:37.320
separating it into its base components. Bottin had&nbsp;
suggested the use of SCP-914 based on the theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.540
that, if this worked correctly, the Clockworks&nbsp;
could extract the Flesh That Hates from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:48.000
infected host, rendering them free of the disease.
The machine made a colossal amount of noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.440
as Clennon hurried back to a safe distance&nbsp;
with the others. But as it was powering up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.600
seconds from activating, Doctor Carpenter noticed&nbsp;
something. The color drained from his face as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.680
saw it; none of the others had noticed from&nbsp;
a distance that the dial hadn’t been set to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.500
Coarse. Instead, it pointed to ‘Very Fine’,&nbsp;
the highest refinement setting SCP-914 had.&nbsp;

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:13.080
“What the hell have you done?” Carpenter yelled&nbsp;
at Clennon, before the machine suddenly activated.&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.400
What stepped free from the Output booth&nbsp;
stunned the researchers. It was a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:24.840
a seemingly-ordinary human, who had presumably&nbsp;
been infected by SCP-610 some time ago. He stepped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:29.280
nervously out of the machine, looking confused&nbsp;
for a moment as a pair of Foundation security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.180
guards cautiously approached him.
“We did it,” Russell said in awe.&nbsp;

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:38.160
Suddenly, fleshy tendrils burst forth from&nbsp;
the man’s arms, latching onto the approaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.840
guards. They screamed in agony, their bodies&nbsp;
melting, becoming a bloody, misshapen mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.700
that stayed attached to the humanoid creature at&nbsp;
the center. It was like they became a part of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:54.420
the guards’ arms and legs forming additional limbs&nbsp;
as their forms were reconstituted and repurposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.400
as part of a monster… that was now crawling&nbsp;
on its six legs towards the research team.&nbsp;

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:04.560
In a blind panic, the group scrambled for the&nbsp;
entrance, the head of the infected man splitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.080
open down the middle, opening up into a wide,&nbsp;
snapping pair of jaws. Panting so hard as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:15.540
ran that his breath fogged up the clear faceplate&nbsp;
of his hazard suit, Russell ran through the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.760
hearing someone close it behind. He stopped&nbsp;
for a moment, looking back to see the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.080
on the other side of the door, starting to&nbsp;
spill through the doorframe like a liquid.&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.340
Running faster around a corner, Russell&nbsp;
came to another halt in the common area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.440
a third security guard following just before a&nbsp;
thick, steel security door came slamming down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.640
sealing the creature on the other side.
“The other door didn’t even hold it. What good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.620
is that going to do?” Researcher David yelled.
“More to the point,” Doctor Carpenter said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.860
turning and marching towards Clennon.
He launched a furious punch at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.820
Assistant, knocking him to the floor.
“Why did you set SCP-914 to Very Fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.220
Clennon?” the enraged doctor shouted. “You’re the&nbsp;
one who unleashed that abomination, you maniac!”&nbsp;

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:00.960
“What?” Clennon replied, fearfully&nbsp;
trembling. “I didn’t, I swear I didn’t!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.760
It said ‘Coarse’, I’m sure of it!”
“You expect us to believe that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.100
a mistake?” Carpenter roared.
“Gentlemen, we need to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:12.840
the situation at hand,” the security officer&nbsp;
interjected. “Regardless of who’s responsible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.880
we need to inform the Foundation higher ups.”
“There’s an emergency lockdown,” Russell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.600
replied. “It would have triggered the&nbsp;
moment the SCP-610 specimen got free.”&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.040
“You’ve trapped us with a monster,&nbsp;
Clennon!” Doctor Carpenter spat.&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:31.200
“Hold on,” Researcher David spoke up. “Wasn’t it&nbsp;
Bottin’s idea to use SCP-914 in the first place?”&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.920
The common area fell silent as everyone&nbsp;
turned to look at Doctor Bottin.&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.600
“Did you plan for this, doc?” David pushed.
Taking off his glasses, Bottin chuckled.&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.800
“Need I remind you, my friends, that&nbsp;
we all agreed to use the Clockworks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.660
did we not? All of our names are on the&nbsp;
request,” he answered. “Besides, accusing me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:52.620
David? That’s rather rich coming from you.”
“What’s that supposed to mean?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:56.100
the researcher fired back.
Beckoning his finger, Bottin encouraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:00.600
the rest of the team to follow him across the&nbsp;
common area, to where a row of lockers stood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:04.860
each door bearing one of their names.&nbsp;
He gestured to the one labelled ‘David’.&nbsp;

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.300
“Go on,” Bottin added.
Shooting him a dirty look, David barged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.640
past the disease expert and opened his locker. Not&nbsp;
one among the group expected what would fall out:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.100
it was an old, leather-bound book, symbols carved&nbsp;
into its front cover. Instantly, thanks to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:26.700
work studying SCP-610, the researchers all knew&nbsp;
a text on Sarkicism when they saw one. It had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.620
long been known that the Flesh That Hates was&nbsp;
often described in texts revered by Sarkic cults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:37.680
who worshiped death, decay, and disease.
“What the hell, David?” Russell exclaimed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.020
picking up the book and examining it in disbelief.
“You’re a Sarkist?” Carpenter asked, the fury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:46.560
in his voice already elevating.
“No, I’m not!” Researcher David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.020
protested. “I’ve never seen that before!”
“It was in your locker!” the doctor yelled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:56.040
“You’re one of those cannibal cultists!&nbsp;
This was your plan? To refine the Flesh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:01.380
for your twisted death-worshipping religion?”
“It certainly seems that way,” Bottin interjected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:06.720
“After all, Researcher David did seem frustrated&nbsp;
at our earlier results. Impatient, even.”&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.700
“You!” David pointed at the doctor.&nbsp;
“You planted this in my locker!”&nbsp;

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.560
“Y-you’re a Sarkist,” Clennon repeated in&nbsp;
a nervous stammer. “You made me turn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.740
dial to Very Fine, you made that thing&nbsp;
appear… you made me your accomplice!”&nbsp;

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:24.240
Angered tears streaming down his face, Clennon’s&nbsp;
hand reached for the security officer’s gun and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:28.740
went to draw it, planning to shoot David on the&nbsp;
spot. But as he went to pull the weapon from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:33.720
guard’s holster, it wouldn’t budge. It was then&nbsp;
that Assistant Clennon stared in horror at his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:39.000
hand; his fingers had fused to the guard’s leg.
The officer gave a twisted grin as his torso&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.340
burst open, tendrils of flesh latching on to&nbsp;
Clennon and assimilating him. Just like it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:49.560
done before, the Flesh creature broke down its&nbsp;
victim’s body, making it a new part of itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:55.020
a grotesque mass of limbs and bloody matter,&nbsp;
littered with the discarded faces of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:59.760
it had infected. The research team looked&nbsp;
at it terrified, their stomachs turning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.380
disgusted at the growing, growling&nbsp;
abomination of bodies fused to one another.&nbsp;

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:10.320
“Oh, you’re beautiful,” Russell heard a voice&nbsp;
whisper behind him; he turned to see Bottin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.960
looking at the SCP-610 creature and smiling.
Researcher David was the closest, and the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:20.700
lunged at him, pulling his leg out from under&nbsp;
him. Screaming in fear, the researcher fell onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:26.940
the ground and was dragged towards the Flesh. It&nbsp;
pulled him in, his body stretching, skin tearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:32.760
and bones snapping as he became part of it, a&nbsp;
new set of limbs added to the monster. Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:38.460
Carpenter went to try and pull him free, but it&nbsp;
was already too late, and he stepped back; knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.320
that, if he touched it, he’d be infected too.
The sound of running caught Russell’s attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.880
and he spotted Bottin making a break for&nbsp;
it. Grabbing Doctor Carpenter by the sleeve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.960
the researcher pulled him in the same&nbsp;
direction, partly running away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.820
Flesh as it assimilated David, and partly&nbsp;
chasing the Sarkic saboteur. The pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.480
them quickly caught up to Bottin, leaning&nbsp;
over himself and panting breathlessly.&nbsp;

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:05.460
“Thank god you two made it,” he wheezed after&nbsp;
the run. “I can’t believe David could do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:10.080
to unleash this… nightmare on us.”
Russell thought to snap at him, to scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:15.540
how dare he keep lying. But Bottin’s back was to&nbsp;
him now, he’d never get a better chance. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:21.000
a cabinet on the corridor wall, containing a fire&nbsp;
axe for emergencies. As carefully and quietly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.260
he could, Russell fished the axe out of its case,&nbsp;
whilst Bottin and Carpenter were looking away.&nbsp;

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.800
“We should get the Foundation to lift&nbsp;
the lockdown,” Bottin was saying. “They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:33.480
need to let us out and then- AAHH!”
He let out a blood-curdling scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:38.160
mid-sentence as the axeblade wounded him. Russell&nbsp;
pulled it back, standing over the doctor as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.920
fell to the floor in agony.
“Russell, have you lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.620
your mind!” Carpenter exclaimed.
“No, doctor. He’s the cultist, I have no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:52.080
how he tricked Clennon, but that Flesh creature&nbsp;
exists because of him,” the researcher answered.&nbsp;

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:56.100
From the floor, unable to move thanks to&nbsp;
his injury, Doctor Bottin began to laugh.&nbsp;

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:02.520
“It took me years to infiltrate the Foundation,&nbsp;
so much time spent waiting to gain access to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:07.800
blessed Flesh!” he gloated. “And now, you’ve&nbsp;
helped me to refine it, to create its ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:14.340
form. You want to kill me? Go ahead, Russell.&nbsp;
My life doesn’t matter. For once that creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:19.620
is free, it will bring about a new age of&nbsp;
Flesh! And Grand Karcist Ion will reward me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:24.420
for the role I played in unleashing it!”
“David was right,” Carpenter realized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:28.500
“Bottin, you… you planted that&nbsp;
book on him to throw us off.”&nbsp;

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:33.000
“Doctor, we need to think of something,”&nbsp;
Researcher Russell interjected, axe still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.560
in hand. “Nobody knows more about the Flesh That&nbsp;
Hates than us, there must be a way to kill that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:41.400
thing. Otherwise, if it gets out of this lockdown,&nbsp;
it could spread to everyone in the Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.240
What about fire? That’s been effective&nbsp;
against SCP-610 infectees before, right?”&nbsp;

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:50.220
“Yes, yes, but…” the doctor desperately tried&nbsp;
to think. “This new being we’ve created-”&nbsp;

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.900
“He created,” Russell argued, looking&nbsp;
at the grinning, wounded Bottin.&nbsp;

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:59.520
“We cannot deny the role we all played&nbsp;
in this, Researcher!” Carpenter argued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:04.800
“And we have no idea if it possesses the same&nbsp;
weaknesses as the Flesh before its refinement.”&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.040
“But we have to try, sir!”&nbsp;
the researcher protested.&nbsp;

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.930
“Of course. I agree.”
***&nbsp;

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:14.880
The sickening sound of a slithering, slick mass&nbsp;
was rapidly approaching. Still laying on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.500
floor in pain, Doctor Bottin had been dragged&nbsp;
to the middle of a junction, with Carpenter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:24.420
Russell hiding around either corner. Suddenly,&nbsp;
Bottin started hollering as the Flesh came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:29.760
into view, practically calling it to him.
“Oh, look at you!” he exclaimed. “You’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:33.720
better than could have ever hoped for!”
Looking down at him with the faces it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:37.980
had collected, the creature stabbed one of&nbsp;
its disgusting appendages through Bottin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:43.200
drawing his body into its growing form.
“I am honored to give my life so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:48.420
the age of Flesh may begin,” the doctor said&nbsp;
weakly through the pain as he was infected.&nbsp;

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:54.000
From around the corner, Carpenter had taken the&nbsp;
Sarkic text and set its pages on fire with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:58.920
cigarette lighter. Turning to the creature, he&nbsp;
hurled the mass of burning paper at it, hoping it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:03.120
would be enough fire to spread to the Flesh while&nbsp;
it was assimilating Bottin, who they had left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:08.220
there as bait. The fiery book collided with the&nbsp;
monstrosity; as Russell peeked around his corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:13.260
he saw the Flesh retreating from the flames,&nbsp;
Bottin’s body now added to it. For a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:17.580
it seemed like the creature was afraid of&nbsp;
the fire, but slowly it drew nearer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:22.140
nearer to the book. With ease, it stamped&nbsp;
on the flames with Bottin’s leg, dousing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:26.640
the fire and suffering no damage to it at all.
Gripping the axe in his hands. Russell went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.380
charge at the creature, only to feel something&nbsp;
push him back. Doctor Carpenter had stopped him.&nbsp;

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:35.040
“Run!” he yelled.
Instantly, Russell turned and raced down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:40.140
corridor, the sound of fleshy tentacles whipping&nbsp;
through the air. He dared not look back, just in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:44.700
case the monster was right behind him. Eventually,&nbsp;
he reached another corner and slammed his back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:49.800
against it, hands shuddering as he held on to&nbsp;
the fire axe. He had no idea what had happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:54.960
to Carpenter. Maybe it got him, or perhaps he had&nbsp;
managed to give it the slip at the last second.&nbsp;

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:59.460
“Russell!” came a hushed voice from around&nbsp;
the corner, one the researcher recognized.&nbsp;

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:04.200
He turned to see Doctor Carpenter approaching him,&nbsp;
sneaking like he was trying to avoid being heard.&nbsp;

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:07.260
“I think I managed to give it&nbsp;
the slip!” the doctor whispered.&nbsp;

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.520
For a second, Russell backed up, his hands&nbsp;
tightening around the axe handle. This might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:16.920
have looked like Doctor Carpenter, but was it?
“Are you really… you, doctor?” he asked.&nbsp;

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:21.840
“Of course, I am!” Carpenter replied. “You&nbsp;
don’t think that… I’m not that monstrosity!”&nbsp;

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:26.160
Russell raised the axe defensively, only&nbsp;
to notice the sadness in the doctor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:30.600
face. Unable to bring himself to swing,&nbsp;
even to find out, the researcher lowered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:34.200
his weapon. Carpenter looked grateful.
“Come on, let’s get them to lift this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:38.040
lockdown,” he said. “I’m sure an MTF can&nbsp;
step in and take care of our mistake.”&nbsp;

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.400
As the pair of them cautiously made their&nbsp;
way towards one of the locked doors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:45.840
Doctor Carpenter reached out a hand&nbsp;
to place it on Russell’s shoulder…&nbsp;

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:51.840
Now go and check out “SCP-610 - The Flesh&nbsp;
That Hates” and “SCP-610 Tale - Aftermath”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:55.560
to experience more unspeakable&nbsp;
horrors of the tainted flesh!

